brief_summary:
The aim of this study will be to evaluate the effect active application on enamel on the 6, 12, 18 and 24-months clinical performance of two universal adhesive systems in non-carious cervical lesions (NCCLs).

Materials \& Methods: 136 restorations will be randomly placed in 34 subjects according to the following groups:

* AU-SEE (Ambar Universal APS adhesive on enamel etched for 15 seconds in the selective enamel etching application strategy);
* AU-AA (Ambar Universal APS adhesive with active application on non-etched enamel);
* AP-SEE (Ambar Universal APS Plus adhesive on enamel etched for 15 seconds in the selective enamel etching application strategy);
* AP-AA (Ambar Universal APS Plus adhesive with active application on non-etched enamel).

All groups will be light-cured for 10s/1,000 mW/cm2. A resin composite will be placed by applying three increments and each one will be light cured for 20s/1,000 mW/cm2. The restorations will be finished immediately with fine diamond burs and polishers. The restorations will be evaluated at baseline and after 6, 12, 18 and 24-months by using the FDI criteria. The following outcomes will be evaluated: retention, marginal staining, marginal adaptation, post-operative sensitivity and recurrence of caries. The differences among the groups will be calculated using Friedman repeated measures analysis of variance rank (α = 0.05).
